296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The second email is created in such a way to remind the user of a new voice message. In addition, the user is asked to download the attachment to access the voicemail. It is a popular phishing method because people tend to ignore the spam and automatically open the file because it was sent as a voicemail notification.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (However, during the inspection, multiple suspicious elements were detected that are typically found in phishing messages. Firstly, the email address of the sender is not related to any voicemail service; therefore, it is necessary to delete such letters from the inbox. The fraudsters used the Voicemail service not to notify the user of a new voicemail but to lure the victim into an attachment. The letter was created in such a way that it looked like an official notification from Voicemail. For example, it includes a caller ID, airtime, and the time of the message. However, the email contains psychological tricks that serve as phishing indicators. The main psychological trick is the sense of urgency. The user may feel that a business call is waiting for them, so they have to open the attachment to check what is in the voicemail. Additionally, the sender has used a fake voicemail notification to attract the attention of the victim.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 176 0 R
/E (The attachment to the message is the most dangerous part of the email. Such type of phishing is dangerous because voices are not typically sent by email, so voice messages are more likely to be spread through a Voicemail app. It can be concluded that the suspicious file may contain malware that can, for example, encrypt the data on the device or steal the personal information of the victim.)
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [6]
>>
