/E (Phishing emails were given the highest priority since they represent the primary threat to the organization. They usually contain the most significant number of indicators of phishing activity. For instance, they can contain several red flags and attachments that can compromise the computer if opened. On the other hand, I classified suspicious emails as those that have some indicators of phishing but are not substantial enough to take any action immediately. Finally, safe emails had some minor indicators of phishing that lacked sufficient evidence to support them. Based on my analysis, out of the five emails, three fall under the phishing category, one suspicious, and one safe. The three phishing emails contained multiple indicators that showed that they aimed to trick the user into providing sensitive information by using social engineering techniques. The other email that I classified as suspicious had no indicators of phishing except the address of the sender, which was not recognized. The last email was safe because it contained no indicators of phishing and had standard organizational email features.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
391 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 186 0 R
/E (The risk classification of phishing emails is critical to the overall email security of an organization. Not only does it enhance the security awareness of the employees, but it also helps the organization respond to phishing emails appropriately. In this case, the classification helped me identify phishing emails, including those that were disguised as legitimate emails. This classification process is vital to enhancing email security since it reduces the risk of being tricked into providing sensitive information to cyber attackers. As a result, organizations should conduct routine email risk assessments and provide continuous security awareness training to employees.)
/Pg 33 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
